  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.7,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["C3"],
      "pins": ["C3.1", "C3.2"],
      "nets": ["unconnected-(C3-Pad1)", "GND"],
      "observed": "C3 is listed as a 0.1 uF capacitor in the EthernetAndPower sheet. Its pin 2 is connected to GND, while pin 1 is only on the single-node net unconnected-(C3-Pad1), so one terminal of the capacitor is floating.",
      "expected": "A 0.1 uF bypass/decoupling capacitor should be connected between a supply rail and ground (for example BAT+ or +3V3 to GND). A capacitor with one pin unconnected does not perform its intended decoupling function and is a connectivity error or missing connection.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(comp (ref \"C3\") (value \"0.1uF\") ... (libsource (lib \"Device\") (part \"C\") (description \"Unpolarized capacitor\")))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"59\") (name \"unconnected-(C3-Pad1)\") ... (node (ref \"C3\") (pin \"1\") (pintype \"passive\")))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"2\") (name \"GND\") ... (node (ref \"C3\") (pin \"2\") (pintype \"passive\")))"},
        {"source": "spice", "quote": "C3 unconnected-_C3-Pad1_ 0 0.1u"}
      ],
      "impact": "If C3 was intended as a populated high-frequency bypass capacitor, it provides no filtering because one terminal is open. This can increase power-rail noise, especially near the battery/USB input or 3.3 V rail, and may make the board behave less reliably than intended.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist and SPICE deck show a 0.1 uF capacitor, C3, with one terminal connected to ground and the other terminal left unconnected, which is inconsistent with normal decoupling-capacitor practice and is likely an open/missing connection anomaly."
}

